            "G190:p0024:b005"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In UML, cardinalities also allow the architect to constrain the number of objects of a certain class that can be related to a single object of the other class. For example, in Figure A-4, the cardinality (1..*) means that a customer must have at least one account, but that there is no upper limit on the number of accounts they can have; the cardinality (1..2) means that an account must be related to at least one, but no more than two customers.",
